      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "Stairs with equal steps"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "A taxi charges 15 for the first km and 8 for each extra km. The fares 15, 23, 31, 39 grow by the same amount each time. Such lists are arithmetic progressions."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "AP",
          "a = 3, d = 5",
          "a, a+d, a+2d",
          "an = a + (n-1)d",
          "check differences",
          "common difference d",
          "n positive integer",
          "which term?"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "An arithmetic progression, or AP, is a list of numbers in which each term is obtained by adding a fixed number d to the previous term.\nThe fixed number d is called the common difference. It can be positive, negative or zero.\nIf the first term is a, the AP is a, a + d, a + 2d, a + 3d and so on.\nTo test whether a list is an AP, check that a2 - a1, a3 - a2, a4 - a3 all give the same value.\nThe nth term of an AP is a sub n = a + (n - 1) d.\nFor the AP 3, 8, 13, 18, the first term is 3 and d = 5, so the 10th term is 3 + 9 x 5 = 48.\nTo find which term equals 78, solve 3 + (n - 1) 5 = 78, so n - 1 = 15 and n = 16.\nIf a term is a fraction or a negative number there is no problem, but n must always be a positive integer."
        }
      },
